# netsurf: one event model for every button and field -- tap sequence, focus, typed events, readystatechange, window load
#
# Hardware run 21: in the claude.ai Google sign-in the code went in but
# "Next" did nothing. Not a per-button bug; the engine lacked pieces of the
# event model every framework builds its buttons on:
#
# - interaction: a tap fires pointerdown, mousedown, (focus moves),
#   pointerup, mouseup, click, in that order. mousedown needed a PRESS flag
#   the click path never carries, so it never fired; pointerup/mouseup came
#   after click. A mousedown handler that cancels keeps focus where it is.
# - focus (vita_polyfill.js, macsurf_qjs_tap_focus): element.focus() and
#   blur() were no-ops and document.activeElement never moved; no
#   focus/blur/focusin/focusout ever reached a page. A tap now focuses the
#   focusable element under it (a form control even under an overlay such as
#   a floating label), a <label> hands focus to its control and toggles a
#   checkbox/radio, anything else clears focus; events carry relatedTarget.
#   `change` on a text field fires when it loses focus with a new value, not
#   per keystroke (box_textarea.c).
# - native events get their interface: MouseEvent / PointerEvent /
#   KeyboardEvent / FocusEvent / InputEvent / WheelEvent, with which,
#   relatedTarget, offsetX/Y, key names and codes; FocusEvent and InputEvent
#   were plain objects without preventDefault, and no interface had
#   prototype.constructor.
# - html_keypress: a page that relayouts on focus (Google's fields) rebuilt
#   the field's box and textarea between the tap and the keyboard's text;
#   the caret is now handed to the same control's new box instead of the
#   keys going nowhere.
# - document.currentScript: the polyfill's getter-only accessor made the
#   engine's own per-script assignment throw (and leave the exception
#   pending) -- currentScript was null for every script.
# - readystatechange fires at document for 'interactive' and 'complete'.
#   Google's Wiz builds a controller whose optional children are absent by
#   waiting for it while readyState is 'loading'; it never came, so the
#   sign-in page's nav controller -- the one behind "Next" -- never existed.
# - window load (compat/macsurf_config.h MACSURF_JS_FIRE_LOAD, repo side):
#   on. No page ever saw window load or readyState 'complete'.
# - document/window dispatchEvent run a snapshot of the listeners with this
#   set (a {once:true} listener removing itself skipped the next one);
#   XMLHttpRequest handlers get an event (e.target.responseText) over a
#   snapshot.
#
# Result (simulator, the run-21 scenario): Next posts
# batchexecute?rpcids=MI613e and Google answers ("Couldn't find this
# account" for the test address), as in Chromium. IN-events (interaction
# pass) checks all of it by tapping; disarms of the once-snapshot (7) and
# the caret hand-over (3, 4) fail it.
